WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.625
Dynamic string libraries reduce some
fixed-buffer problems by managing storage

2
00:00:04.705 --> 00:00:05.722
growth internally.

3
00:00:05.802 --> 00:00:08.671
Instead of asking every caller to predict
the final size,

4
00:00:08.751 --> 00:00:12.219
a dynamic string object can grow as
content is appended or formatted.

5
00:00:12.299 --> 00:00:16.424
That can improve maintainability,
especially for complex string construction.

6
00:00:16.504 --> 00:00:19.015
Dynamic growth does not remove failure
handling.

7
00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:22.025
Allocation can fail, a configured maximum
can be reached,

8
00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:23.958
or a builder can enter an error state.

9
00:00:24.038 --> 00:00:28.044
Code should know how the library reports
failure and whether the object remains

10
00:00:28.124 --> 00:00:30.456
usable, becomes frozen, or must be
discarded.

11
00:00:30.536 --> 00:00:33.824
Continuing with a failed builder can
produce misleading output.

12
00:00:33.904 --> 00:00:35.817
Ownership transfer must be explicit.

13
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:39.006
A dynamic string may own its buffer, lend
a temporary pointer,

14
00:00:39.086 --> 00:00:42.972
transfer ownership to the caller, or
invalidate earlier views when it grows.

15
00:00:43.052 --> 00:00:46.998
Those rules should be documented in code
patterns and followed consistently.

16
00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:51.323
A pointer returned from a dynamic string
object should not be treated as permanently

17
00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:53.974
stable unless the library promises that
behavior.

18
00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:56.505
Cleanup responsibility needs the same
clarity.

19
00:00:56.585 --> 00:00:59.215
If a function creates a dynamic string,
who frees it?

20
00:00:59.295 --> 00:01:01.029
If ownership is handed to a caller,

21
00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:03.859
is that visible in the function name or
documentation?

22
00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:06.450
If an error occurs halfway through
construction,

23
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.982
are all allocated pieces released exactly
once?

24
00:01:09.062 --> 00:01:11.633
Maximum length policy is part of defensive
design.

25
00:01:11.713 --> 00:01:14.941
Dynamic strings can grow beyond what a
fixed buffer would allow,

26
00:01:15.021 --> 00:01:18.309
which is useful when the data is
legitimate and bounded by policy.

27
00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:22.574
Without a maximum, a builder can consume
more memory than the application intends.

28
00:01:22.654 --> 00:01:26.660
Safer dynamic use includes size limits,
allocation checks, and cleanup tests.
